  "text_sanskrit": "साङ्ख्ययोगौ पृथग्बालाः प्रवदन्ति न पण्डिताः । एकमप्यास्थितः सम्यगुभयोर्विन्दते फलम् ॥ ५-४ ॥",
  "itrans": "sA~Nkhyayogau pR^ithagbAlAH pravadanti na paNDitAH . ekamapyAsthitaH samyagubhayorvindate phalam .. 5-4 ..",
  "translation": "Sankhya and Yoga are, the young say, separate (from each other), but not the wise; he who properly observes (or follows) even one obtains the fruit of both. (4)\nV. Sankhya means knowledge. The latter half clears the doubt that Yoga might not lead to knowledge. If any one of the two courses be followed, the man attains to the results of both. (4)\nA. It might be supposed that either course is independently capable of leading one to certain common results and that the previous statement does not expressly show that Yoga leads to wisdom. To remove this mistake the Lord Himself explains its meaning in the next.",
